  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/b03beb34efa3fef7aaf18a879eb628748d86dc1b47eb4b45f60c4d02935e372b.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(10,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(10,6),$ we substitute $x=10$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10-4)^{2} & =4p(6+3) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+3) \\\\ 4 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$",
